# Routh I.Art.92 — impacts cannot move the centre of gravity
# — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "A Treatise on Dynamics of a Particle" (Cambridge, 1898),
# Part I, Art. 92: "A free system of mutually attracting particles is in
# motion. Prove (1) that the centre of gravity moves in a straight line with
# uniform velocity, and (2) that the motion of the centre of gravity is not
# affected by any impacts between the particles."
#
# WHY IT HOLDS. Art. 93 gives the reason in one line: "the effect of the
# mutual action of two particles (whether attracting or impinging on each
# other) is to transfer a momentum from one to the other". Every internal
# action -- gravitational or percussive -- appears twice, equal and opposite,
# so it cancels from the sum. The total linear momentum is therefore an exact
# constant, and since the total momentum IS the total mass times the velocity
# of the centre of gravity, that centre must move uniformly in a straight line
# no matter how violent the interior becomes.
#
# WHAT MAKES IT A REAL TEST. This notebook runs BOTH kinds of internal action
# at once -- pairwise gravity (G = 1) and hard elastic collisions -- so the
# particles genuinely attract, swing past one another, and strike. The
# trajectories are complicated and chaotic; the centre of gravity is not.
#
# THE NUMBERS. Four unit-mass spheres of radius 0.3:
#
#   obj0  at (-1.5,  0.2, 0)  moving ( 1.2,  0.0, 0)
#   obj1  at ( 1.5, -0.2, 0)  moving (-0.8,  0.0, 0)
#   obj2  at ( 0.0,  1.5, 0)  moving ( 0.0, -1.0, 0)
#   obj3  at ( 0.0, -1.5, 0)  moving ( 0.0,  0.9, 0)
#
# Total mass M = 4. Total momentum P = (0.4, -0.1, 0), which is conserved
# exactly. Centre of gravity at t = 0 is the origin, so for all time
#
#     COM(t) = P/M * t = (0.1*t, -0.025*t, 0)
#
# and in particular COM(5) = (0.5, -0.125, 0) exactly. The cells compute the
# centre of gravity directly as the mean of the four positions (legitimate
# because the masses are equal) and print its distance from that prediction.
#
# Note what is NOT conserved: total energy falls whenever a collision is
# inelastic, and the individual paths are unpredictable. Momentum and the
# centre of gravity do not care.
#
# SETUP NOTE. The collision radii also serve a numerical purpose: hard spheres
# cannot pass through each other, so no pair can reach the r -> 0 singularity
# of the 1/r^2 attraction. The system stays well conditioned however long it
# runs.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: four bodies falling together under their own gravity,
# scattering off each other, and flying apart on complicated paths -- while
# their mean position slides steadily along one straight line at constant
# speed, exactly as if nothing had happened.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p1_centre_of_gravity
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_centre_of_gravity.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p1_centre_of_gravity.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 1
set system.rtol = 1e-12
set system.atol = 1e-14
collide on

new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.3, position = [-1.5,  0.2, 0], velocity = [ 1.2,  0,   0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.3, position = [ 1.5, -0.2, 0], velocity = [-0.8,  0,   0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.3, position = [ 0,    1.5, 0], velocity = [ 0,   -1.0, 0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.3, position = [ 0,   -1.5, 0], velocity = [ 0,    0.9, 0], restitution = 1 }

# total momentum (0.4, -0.1, 0) and the centre of gravity at the origin
momentum
(obj0.position + obj1.position + obj2.position + obj3.position) / 4

run 5 steps 10

# how many impacts happened, and the momentum afterwards -- unchanged
system.collisions
momentum
# the centre of gravity, and its distance from the predicted (0.5, -0.125, 0)
(obj0.position + obj1.position + obj2.position + obj3.position) / 4
norm((obj0.position + obj1.position + obj2.position + obj3.position) / 4 - [0.5, -0.125, 0])
reset

set system.g_constant = 1
set system.rtol = 1e-12
set system.atol = 1e-14
collide on
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.3, position = [-1.5,  0.2, 0], velocity = [ 1.2,  0,   0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.3, position = [ 1.5, -0.2, 0], velocity = [-0.8,  0,   0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.3, position = [ 0,    1.5, 0], velocity = [ 0,   -1.0, 0], restitution = 1 }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.3, position = [ 0,   -1.5, 0], velocity = [ 0,    0.9, 0], restitution = 1 }
momentum

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.02
